## Status

The upstream half of this finding is already owned by **git-erg 0277, 0278 and
0279**, filed by a sibling session on 2026-09-10 between 06:53 and 09:40 and
merged the same morning. No fourth ticket was filed. This ticket keeps the
harness half and the four observations 0277 does not carry.

Author verdict, 2026-09-10: **equality here, size budget upstream.** The
harness copy is to be gated by a checksum test against the asset embedded in
the vendored `tickets/erg`, not by a size budget of its own; harness-local
practice moves into `rules/`, where the existing gate reaches it.
`chemin-de-voix` is deferred.

## Context

`tickets/AGENTS.md` is not a harness file. It is an asset embedded in the `erg`
binary (`git-erg/src/go/assets/AGENTS.md`, mapped by `bootstrap_assets.go`) and
written by `erg init`. This repo has been editing it as if it owned it: 7 969
chars against a shipped 2 141, five additive edits between 2026-07-11 and
2026-09-01, none propagated upstream.

**How exposed that fork is, stated at the evidence level actually reached.**
`migrate.go:261` calls `installAssets(root, migrateAssetPaths, false, false)`
with `refuseDiverged=false`, and `migrate_test.go:448` asserts at unit level
that "a diverged AGENTS.md IS force-overwritten by migrateLayout (charter
behaviour)". End to end the path is **not** demonstrated: git-erg 0278 records
a fixture carrying a local edit that survived `erg migrate`, because the run
reported `already clean` and never reached the asset step. So the hazard is
real in the code and open in practice — whether it fires on a store that
genuinely needs migrating is git-erg 0278's first action, not a settled fact.
An earlier revision of this ticket asserted the overwrite as established; that
overstated it.

**The adopters are current, not frozen.** Nine adopter copies are byte-identical
to the shipped asset (md5 `b622d4f0…`, 2 141 chars), and this ticket first read
that as staleness. Measured properly — git-erg 0278 ran `erg init --dry-run`
across eleven repos rather than inferring from hashes — six are `unchanged`,
four `would refresh` and have since been refreshed, and one (`chemin-de-voix`)
has no provenance stamp at all. Byte-identical to the shipped asset means
current. The problem is what the shipped asset *says*, not how old it is; 0278
reaches the same conclusion independently.

## Four observations 0277 does not carry

**1. The `erg-github` sentence is a mood error, not a fiction.** The shipped
asset states, in the indicative, that `tickets/erg-github` "adds a `verify`
check that fails a PR referencing a still-open ticket". The helper is real — a
5 471-byte POSIX script with `install` and `verify` verbs — and does exactly
that. It is installed in **zero** repos on this machine: the script exists only
in git-erg's own `tickets/`, and `erg-verify.yml` is in no workflows directory
anywhere, git-erg's included. Every adopter therefore reads that a required
check exists when none does. The harness dropped the sentence in `9703af3`
under the label "erg-github fiction"; that label is imprecise and this ticket
repeated it. The repair is the mood — describe an optional facility as
optional — not deletion.

**2. The other shipped claim is under-generalized, not wrong.** "On ID
collision, renumber to the next free ID" works, and in a single-session repo it
is correct. It races only under parallel sessions. The frontier rule is its
generalization; the shipped line is the common special case.

**3. This repo lost a rule the asset still carries.** The fork dropped the
anti-suffix bullet — "Ticket IDs are atomic 4-digit numbers -- never suffix
them (`0134a`, `0134b`)" — when it rewrote that bullet into the optimistic-ID
one. Nine adopters have it; this repo does not. The drift is not one-way.

**4. Removing the forge scan breaks a live cross-reference.**
`rules/coding-bash.md` cites `tickets/AGENTS.md` as the file stating the general
form of "a check whose all-clear is indistinguishable from 'I could not look'".
Moving that material must repoint the citation.

## Resident cost, and the gate that does not reach it

`AGENTS.md` is resident in every session via `@tickets/AGENTS.md` in
`CLAUDE.md`, a fact `rules/README.md` states explicitly. At 7 969 chars that is
~2 000 tokens (chars/4) on top of the 35 926-char gated set whose remaining
headroom is 74 chars. `tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` scopes to
`RULES = REPO / "rules"`, so none of its three invariants reach it. Applied by
hand:

- `test_resident_rules_carry_no_ticket_numbers` — **would fail**, 2 hits
  (`ticket 0321`, `ticket 0243`).
- `test_resident_rules_ship_no_shell_recipes` — **would pass while the file
  carries a shell recipe.** `SHELL_FENCE` anchors the opening fence at column
  zero; the block at `AGENTS.md:41-45` is indented two spaces under a list item.
  Measured against that known-positive: anchored 0 hits, whitespace-tolerant
  1 hit. This blind spot is independent of this ticket and applies to `rules/**`.

## The candidate, measured

A merged version was drafted to price the question rather than argue it: every
durable local idea upstreamed, all incident narrative stripped, the anti-suffix
rule restored, the `erg-github` sentence put in the conditional.

| | chars | ~tokens (chars/4) |
|---|-------|-------------------|
| shipped asset | 2 141 | 535 |
| candidate | 3 873 | 968 |
| this repo today | 7 969 | 1 992 |

Tool-invariant, worth upstreaming: optimistic allocation with no reservation;
renumber clear of the frontier while allocating dense; re-check the seat at the
merge gate; `erg check` against the default branch rather than the branch;
decisions are not artifacts; the handoff-document sections. Harness-local,
staying here: the `gh pr view` scan (a shell recipe, and `erg` core is offline
and forge-blind by design) and the CI paragraph naming this repo's jobs and
scripts.

## The open conflict

0277 records an author decision of the same day that the displaced content goes
to the **on-demand channel** (`erg spec` / `erg integration`) and explicitly
**not** into `assets/AGENTS.md`: "folding 5 900 bytes into the shipped asset
makes every adopter pay ~1 500 resident tokens for war stories most of them
will never hit." The verdict recorded above points the other way, at a size
budget on the asset.

They may reconcile: 0277 refuses 5 900 bytes of narrative, while the candidate
adds 1 730 bytes of rules with the narrative removed, ~430 tokens. The middle
is compact rules in the asset, long-form recovery on demand. That is an
arbitration for the author, and no upstream work should start before it.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/AGENTS.md` — the forked copy, 7 969 chars
- `rules/README.md` — names AGENTS.md as resident-by-another-path
- `rules/coding-bash.md` — cites AGENTS.md for the all-clear form; see observation 4
- `tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py` — the three invariants, scoped to `rules/`
- git-erg `0277` (ban and destination), `0278` (asset machinery, blocks 0277), `0279` (direction-blind downgrade)

## Actions

1. Get the arbitration on the open conflict above. Nothing upstream starts first.
2. Wait on git-erg 0278, then 0277: the harness copy cannot return to pristine
   until the asset carries whatever the arbitration puts there.
3. Move harness-local practice out of `tickets/AGENTS.md` into `rules/`: the
   `gh pr view` recipe to the script or skill that runs it, the CI paragraph to
   a rules body, the two ticket numbers to named memory pointers. Repoint the
   `rules/coding-bash.md` citation in the same commit.
4. Return `tickets/AGENTS.md` to the shipped asset, byte for byte.
5. Add the equality gate: a test asserting `tickets/AGENTS.md` matches the asset
   embedded in the vendored `tickets/erg`. Prefer it over a size budget — it
   makes drift impossible rather than expensive, and it self-heals after any
   path that reinstalls the asset.
6. Fix `SHELL_FENCE` to tolerate leading whitespace (observation, independent).
7. `chemin-de-voix`, deferred: it is a tool upgrade, not a doc fix. Its `erg` is
   the 2026-05-18 build and its four tickets use `Tag:`, so its AGENTS.md is
   consistent with the binary it runs. Update the binary, migrate the headers,
   and the asset is rewritten by the tool. Blocked in practice by two unpushed
   commits on its `master` (2 ahead, 1 behind `origin/master`) that belong to
   someone else.

## Test

Red step, before any prose moves: the equality assertion. Extract the embedded
asset from the vendored `tickets/erg` and compare it to `tickets/AGENTS.md`.
Against today's tree it must fail on a 5 828-char difference; against a
pristine copy it must pass. Run both arms — an equality test that cannot fail
is indistinguishable from one that never read either file.

Second, independent: relax `SHELL_FENCE` to tolerate leading whitespace and
watch it go red on `AGENTS.md:41`, while staying green on today's `rules/**`,
so the relaxation is proved to change only what it should.

## Verification

- [ ] the equality test is red against today's tree and green after the trim
- [ ] `rules/coding-bash.md`'s citation resolves to wherever the all-clear form ended up
- [ ] the anti-suffix rule is present in whatever this repo ends up reading
- [ ] no rule listed under "the candidate, measured" is lost — each is
      upstreamed, relocated to `rules/`, or in a memory note something cites
- [ ] `uv run pytest -m adherence` green, `erg check tickets/` PASS

## Invariants

- No rule currently in `tickets/AGENTS.md` is lost
- The gated resident total stays under `RESIDENT_BUDGET`; relocating prose into
  `rules/` without trimming it moves the cost inside the gate rather than removing it
- No adopter repo is edited from this repo's worktree
- No upstream change is made before the arbitration; 0277 is Blocked-by 0278

## Exit criteria

- Arbitration recorded in this log
- `tickets/AGENTS.md` matches the shipped asset, and a test fails when it stops
- Harness-local practice lives in `rules/`, under the existing gate
- The four observations above are landed upstream or recorded as declined
- Closes only after git-erg 0277 merges and this repo's copy is re-checked
